    "created_at": "2016-02-08T09:45:21Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-02-08T09:45:21Z",
    "body": "> 1. Change processing such that having child elements (but presumably not child comments or multiple child text nodes) is a failure, as suggested by @sideshowbarker in https://github.com/validator/validator/issues/238#issuecomment-181226538\n> 2. Drop the \"A `time` element that does not have a `datetime` content attribute must not have any element descendants\" authoring requirement.\n\nIf we do option 1, I think we should in fact make parsing of `time` fail if any child comments are found.\n\n> I think I prefer option 2\n\nAfter thinking about it some more, I’m also leaning toward option 2 (dropping the restriction). But I’d first like to hear what @Hixie thinks, since I think he’s the one who added the restriction to being with, and hopefully remembers the rationale. So it would be good to know if he still thinks it’s useful, and why.\n\nIn general for existing spec requirements, I tend to assume they were all added for a good reason and we should be careful to not change them without careful consideration. I think it’s possible @Hixie added the restriction originally to try to help authors avoid a potential problem, and in this current discussion we just haven’t yet looked at it from the same perspective that he did initially when he added the restriction.\n\n> because a `textContent` primitive (the concatenation of all _descendant_ text nodes) is something that even non-browsers could trivially implement. The concatenation of all _child_ text nodes is only barely simpler.\n\n100% agreed—it’s trivial to implement the equivalent a `textContent`. So in hindsight I think I was a bit off base in some of my comments over in https://github.com/validator/validator/issues/238#issuecomment-181176954 as far as my speculation about the original rationale for the restriction. Any concern about parsing probably didn’t figure into it much, since as you point out, it’s hard to imagine an implementation where it would present much of any extra work to implement support for `time` contents with element children.\n\nFor example, in the case of the validator code, which doesn’t use a DOM but instead a streaming SAX API, I already do the equivalent of grabbing just the `textContent`, easily. In fact at this point I would need to add additional code to have it fail for the case when a `datetime`-less `time` has  child element content.\n\nSo if I were to think of it purely from the perspective of my own implementation, I’d actually rather allow child elements in `time`, instead of needing to do any additional work (which arguably I should have already done for the case of the validator, where the behavior should be fully checking against the stricter document-conformance/authoring requirements and not the more-liberal processing requirements).\n\nBut anyway I think the issue of relative implementation difficulty should not be a concern here either way on whether we should add an additional the processing requirements (option 1) or instead just drop the existing document-conformance restriction (option 2). I think the choice should rightly be based on what’s best for authors. But a problem for me at least in trying to evaluate that is, I don’t actually understand well what the use cases are for `time`, and how authors are using it and what they want to do with it.\n\nSo I think we might want to try to get some authors to weigh in on this who are actually using `time`, and find out what they think (along with getting @Hixie’s current thinking on it).\n\nDocument-conformance requirements are in many or most cases a gray area—they’re judgement calls that try to strike a balance between trying to help authors not shoot themselves in the foot—helping authors catch potential mistakes—versus not being unnecessarily prescriptive.\n\nIt may be that in the case of the current restriction that on balance it’s being overly prescriptive and not really helping authors avoid potential problems. Or it may be that it’s helping avoid a potential problem that we haven’t considered in this discussion yet but that @Hixie did originally.\n",
